(>> 5.2 Curvilinear Motion: Normal and Tangential Coordinates) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(When a particle travels along a curved trajectory, its acceleration vector is naturally) Tj
T*
(decoupled into orthogonal components along the tangent \(t\) and principal normal \(n\) to) Tj
T*
(the path:) Tj
T*
(* Tangential Velocity: v = ds/dt \(always directed tangentially to the curve\).) Tj
T*
(* Tangential Acceleration: a_t = dv/dt = v * \(dv/ds\). Represents rate of change of) Tj
T*
(speed \(magnitude\).) Tj
T*
(* Normal \(Centripetal\) Acceleration: a_n = v^2 / rho, where rho is local radius of) Tj
T*
(curvature: rho = [ 1 + \(dy/dx\)^2 ]^\(3/2\) / |d^2y/dx^2|. Represents rate of change of) Tj
T*
(direction \(always directed inward toward the center of curvature\).) Tj
T*
(* Total Acceleration Magnitude: a = sqrt\( a_t^2 + a_n^2 \). Direction angle: phi =) Tj
T*
(atan\( a_n / a_t \).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
/F2 9.5 Tf
(>> 5.3 Kinetics of Particles: Newton's Second Law & D'Alembert's Principle) Tj
T*
/F1 9 Tf
() Tj
T*
(* Newton's Second Law: Sigma F = m * a. In Cartesian components: Sigma Fx = m * a_x;) Tj
T*
(Sigma Fy = m * a_y. In path coordinates: Sigma F_t = m * a_t; Sigma F_n = m * \(v^2 /) Tj
T*
(rho\).) Tj
T*
(* D'Alembert's Principle: Converts a dynamic acceleration problem into an equivalent) Tj
T*
(static equilibrium problem by introducing a fictitious Inertia Force: F_inertia = - m) Tj
T*
(* a.) Tj
T*
(Dynamic Equilibrium Equation: Sigma F + \(- m * a\) = 0.) Tj
T*
(Engineers can then apply standard equations of static equilibrium \(Sigma Fx = 0, Sigma) Tj
T*
