(Problem Context: A construction muck cart of mass m = 800 kg travels at v1 = 15 m/s) Tj
T*
(toward an emergency arresting spring buffer of stiffness k = 60 kN/m \(60,000 N/m\). If) Tj
T*
(the track is horizontal and rolling resistance is neglected, calculate: \(1\) Maximum) Tj
T*
(compression x_max of the spring buffer in bringing the cart to rest, \(2\) Maximum) Tj
T*
(deceleration experienced.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 1: Work-Energy Equation \(Sigma U_1-2 = Delta KE\):) Tj
T*
(Initial state 1 \(before contact\): v1 = 15 m/s => KE_1 = 0.5 * m * v1^2 = 0.5 * 800 *) Tj
T*
(\(15\)^2 = 400 * 225 = 90,000 J \(90 kJ\).) Tj
T*
(Final state 2 \(at maximum spring compression\): v2 = 0 m/s => KE_2 = 0 J.) Tj
T*
(Work done by arresting spring on cart during compression: U_spring = - 0.5 * k * x_max^2) Tj
T*
(= - 0.5 * 60,000 * x_max^2 = - 30,000 * x_max^2.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 2: Equate Work to Change in Kinetic Energy:) Tj
T*
(- 30,000 * x_max^2 = 0 - 90,000 => 30,000 * x_max^2 = 90,000.) Tj
T*
(x_max^2 = 90,000 / 30,000 = 3.0 => x_max = sqrt\(3.0\) = 1.732 m.) Tj
T*
(Solution Step 3: Compute Maximum Deceleration:) Tj
T*
(Maximum retarding force occurs at maximum compression: F_max = k * x_max = 60,000 *) Tj
T*
(1.732 = 103,923 N.) Tj
T*
(a_max = F_max / m = 103,923 N / 800 kg = 129.90 m/s^2 \(~ 13.2 g\).) Tj
T*
(Conclusion: Maximum spring compression x_max = 1.732 m; maximum deceleration = 129.90) Tj
T*
(m/s^2.) Tj
